## Why This Is Asked

This question tests designing an extensible authorization policy system that can evolve without touching core logic, mirroring real-world gateways.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy for concrete policies (RBAC, Time, Attribute)
- Composite for AND/OR
- Registry/Factory for runtime loading
- Thread-safety and immutability in evaluators

## Code Example

```typescript
interface Policy {
  evaluate(ctx: any): boolean;
}

class Registry {
  private factories = new Map<string, () => Policy>();
  register(name: string, factory: () => Policy) { this.factories.set(name, factory); }
  get(name: string): Policy { return this.factories.get(name)!(); }
}

class AndPolicy implements Policy {
  constructor(private a: Policy, private b: Policy) {}
  evaluate(ctx: any){ return this.a.evaluate(ctx) && this.b.evaluate(ctx); }
}

// Usage
const registry = new Registry();
registry.register('rbac', () => new RolePolicy(['admin']));
registry.register('time', () => new TimeWindowPolicy('09:00-17:00'));
const policy = new AndPolicy(registry.get('rbac'), registry.get('time'));
policy.evaluate({ user: 'admin', time: '10:00' });
```

